VS Code's built-in search is actually very good for this — no Copilot needed.

  Fastest method — VS Code Search (Ctrl+Shift+F):

  1. Open the folder containing your SAS files
  2. Ctrl+Shift+F opens the global search panel
  3. In the "files to include" box, type: *.sas
  4. Search for each mnemonic — e.g., FTH or FIRST TIME HOMEBUYER
  5. Results show every file and line that matches, clickable

  Power moves in that search panel:
  - Toggle the .* button to enable regex — then you can search for multiple mnemonics at once: FTH|MOM|ESV|MRF|NMI
  - Use the "files to exclude" box to skip logs, backups, etc.: *.log, *backup*

  From the terminal (if you prefer CLI):

  # Search all .sas files for a mnemonic
  findstr /s /i "FTH" *.sas

  # Or multiple at once
  findstr /s /i "FTH MOM ESV MRF" *.sas

  The batch approach — search all 31 at once:

  findstr /s /i /c:"FTH" /c:"MOM" /c:"ESV" /c:"MRF" /c:"NMI" /c:"RSS" /c:"TAO" /c:"RMG" /c:"VCN" /c:"IRI" /c:"O2P"
  /c:"CRV" /c:"IDE" /c:"ZHR" /c:"PFS" /c:"PRA" /c:"DAR" /c:"VSX" /c:"RCR" /c:"COB" /c:"VAW" /c:"GNE" /c:"COR" /c:"VUI"
  /c:"PVE" /c:"MOS" /c:"RAT" /c:"MFY" /c:"MMT" /c:"IFC" /c:"VBA_LTA" *.sas

  Also worth knowing: If you install Claude Code (npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code), you can run it right in VS
  Code's terminal, point it at that SAS directory, and it'll do the searching, extracting, and cataloging for you
  programmatically. That's probably the biggest time saver given what you're trying to do.
